drills.explanation:
Wrap the whole request in `try`/`finally`, so every exit clears the context, and translate access-denied failures by who is calling:

```java
try {
    String authorization = request.headers().get("Authorization");
    if (authorization != null) {
        String user = authorization.startsWith("Bearer ") ? tokens.get(authorization.substring(7)) : null;
        if (user == null) return new Response(401, Map.of("WWW-Authenticate", "Bearer error=\"invalid_token\""), "");
        SecurityContext.set(user);
    }
    try { return new Response(200, Map.of(), controller.handle(request)); }
    catch (AccessDeniedException denied) {
        return SecurityContext.current() == null ? new Response(401, Map.of("WWW-Authenticate", "Bearer"), "") : new Response(403, Map.of(), "");
    }
} finally {
    SecurityContext.clear();
}
```

The tests catch the plausible wrong filters: no `finally` (the next request on the thread is served as `ana`), clearing only after a normal return (the throwing controller leaves `ana` behind), treating a forged token as anonymous (the controller runs), and answering 403 to an anonymous caller (it should be asked to authenticate).

